WEBVTT

NOTE Generated by docs/tutorial/capture.mjs

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:09.307
HoMM3 Board Game Scenario Creator
A step-by-step tutorial: write a Scenario, build its map, export it for the Mission Book.

2
00:00:10.026 --> 00:00:18.867
Welcome
The Scenario Creator runs in your browser, online at aliksumin.github.io/HOMMIII_BG_MapCreator. Everything you make stays in this browser until you export it.

3
00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:30.914
Five Scenario types
Clash (player against player), Cooperative, Alliance, Campaign and Solo (one player and a story book). The type sets the heading and where the file goes in the Mission Book.

4
00:00:30.914 --> 00:00:39.316
The template
New here? Open the template: a finished Campaign Scenario that uses nearly every feature, to explore step by step.

5
00:00:39.316 --> 00:00:50.627
Continue or open a file
Projects you worked on are listed under Continue. You can also open a saved .scenario.json project, or a .map file from the community map editor.

6
00:00:50.627 --> 00:00:55.463
Start a Clash
For this demo we start a Clash for two or three players.

7
00:00:55.463 --> 00:01:10.659
The editor
Ten steps on the left, the form in the middle, and the live Mission Book page on the right. A red mark on a step means something there still needs filling in.

8
00:01:10.665 --> 00:01:14.637
Name the Scenario
The name becomes the heading and the file name.

9
00:01:14.637 --> 00:01:17.715
File name
Below the field you see the .tex file name the Mission Book will get.

10
00:01:17.715 --> 00:01:28.222
Live page
The page preview on the right updates with every change. Hide page gives the form more room.

11
00:01:28.222 --> 00:01:38.203
Type and heading
You can still change the type here. Campaigns add a campaign name, scenario number and faction; Solo sets one player.

12
00:01:38.203 --> 00:01:48.637
Heading line and title lines
Leave the heading line empty for the standard one. Use two title lines for a long name.

13
00:01:48.637 --> 00:01:51.447
Difficulty rating
An optional badge in the corner of the heading.

14
00:01:51.447 --> 00:01:58.861
Author
Your name, and optionally where the Scenario first appeared, with a link.

15
00:01:58.861 --> 00:02:08.437
Title icon
Pick the round icon left of the heading from the Mission Book's library, or upload your own PNG.

16
00:02:10.036 --> 00:02:23.376
Flavor text
One or two paragraphs that set the scene. They print in italics under the Author line.

17
00:02:23.376 --> 00:02:35.378
The story page
Everything below prints on its own page after the map, and all of it is optional: the long story, Story Locations, Special Tiles and numbered Paragraphs.

18
00:02:35.378 --> 00:02:49.989
A story book
Campaign and Solo Scenarios can tell their story like a story book: a location or a special Field sends the reader to a numbered paragraph, written §12 or picked from the § Read menu.

19
00:02:49.989 --> 00:03:01.200
Paragraphs
Paragraphs end with choices that point on. Moving or removing one renumbers every reference. Our Clash leaves the story book empty, so it prints nothing.

20
00:03:02.846 --> 00:03:11.865
Length and players
10 Rounds, for 2 or 3 players. The page prints it as “2 or 3”.

21
00:03:11.865 --> 00:03:23.726
Resources
Starting Resources per resource, and Starting Income as a position on the income track. They start at the values of the Mission Book's templates.

22
00:03:23.726 --> 00:03:33.365
Starting Units
Add lines yourself or click a suggestion. Lines take the text markup, so icons like Bronze print as game icons.

23
00:03:33.365 --> 00:03:38.589
Town Buildings
Tick the buildings every player starts with; a Bronze Dwelling is the usual start.

24
00:03:38.589 --> 00:03:50.486
Tile pool and Bonus
Turn on the Map Tile Pool if players place random tiles while exploring. The Bonus line takes one option, or several to choose from.

25
00:03:53.899 --> 00:04:03.573
The tile box
Face-down tiles first (Starting I, Far II–III, Near IV–V, Center VI–VII, plus water and underground), then every printed tile of every expansion.

26
00:04:03.573 --> 00:04:10.839
Click, then click the board
Click a tile to pick it up, then click the board to place it. The outline shows whether it fits.

27
00:04:10.839 --> 00:04:15.567
Drag and drop
Or drag a tile from the box straight onto the board.

28
00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:23.797
Place several
A picked tile stays in your hand, so you can place several. Esc puts it down.

29
00:04:23.797 --> 00:04:33.419
Printed tiles
Search the box by tile number or terrain, or filter by expansion, and place a printed tile where the Scenario needs a specific one.

30
00:04:33.419 --> 00:04:45.194
One of each printed tile
Place a printed tile twice and the editor warns, in the panel and in the status bar: the game has only one of each.

31
00:04:45.194 --> 00:04:57.759
Both copies in red
With nothing selected, both copies are outlined in red. Select the extra one and press Del to remove it.

32
00:05:02.295 --> 00:05:10.631
The status bar
It counts the tiles by kind and warns when the map falls apart into unconnected parts, like now.

33
00:05:10.631 --> 00:05:16.782
Move a tile
Drag a placed tile to move it. It snaps to the hex grid.

34
00:05:16.782 --> 00:05:24.112
Rotate
Select a tile and press R to rotate it (Shift+R the other way), or use the Rotate buttons in the panel on the right.

35
00:05:24.112 --> 00:05:34.098
Navigate the board
Drag the empty board to pan, use the mouse wheel or these buttons to zoom, and the last button fits the whole map.

36
00:05:36.628 --> 00:05:44.640
The inspector
Select a tile to edit it: rotate it, and say what stands on each of its seven Fields.

37
00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:51.818
Fields
Click a Field on the map, or pick it in the little flower, then put things on it.

38
00:05:51.819 --> 00:06:03.855
Cubes
A cube in a player's colour on a face-down Starting Tile marks whose start it is, as published Mission Book maps do. A Black Cube marks a location as already visited.

39
00:06:03.855 --> 00:06:07.529
Faction borders
A face-down Starting Tile can also get a faction border. Our players choose their own Factions, so the tiles stay plain.

40
00:06:07.529 --> 00:06:11.148
Heroes
A Hero portrait marks where a set Hero stands, such as a Campaign's AI Hero. In a Clash the players bring their own.

41
00:06:11.148 --> 00:06:20.807
Location tokens
Monoliths, Whirlpools and Subterranean Gates go on Fields as tokens, always with their partner: here a two-way Monolith pair joins the two flanks.

42
00:06:20.807 --> 00:06:33.431
Creature Banks
With the optional Creature Banks rule, Far and Near Tiles can hold a bank. This Random (Far) token says: when the tile is revealed, draw a Far bank for its Blocked Field.

43
00:06:33.431 --> 00:06:49.000
Blocked Fields
Mark a Field the Scenario closes off: here the flood covers the Mystical Garden of C4 until a Timed Event opens it. Suggested placement draws a tile faded, as a hint.

44
00:06:49.938 --> 00:07:02.662
Layouts
A Scenario can have several map layouts, for example one per player count. Each has a name, the player counts it is for, and a caption under the image.

45
00:07:02.662 --> 00:07:08.299
.map and PNG
Export .map saves the layout in the community map editor's format (for assets/map-files/); Export PNG renders the map image (for assets/maps/).

46
00:07:08.299 --> 00:07:16.157
A second layout
Add a layout for three players, then fill it from the .map file we just saved.

47
00:07:16.157 --> 00:07:22.636
Import .map
Paste the text from the community map editor, or open a .map file. Replace the layout or add the tiles to it.

48
00:07:22.636 --> 00:07:34.464
The third player
The three-player layout gets a third face-down Starting Tile, with a cube in the third player's colour.

49
00:07:34.464 --> 00:07:37.679
Layout tabs
Switch layouts with the tabs. Duplicate copies the current layout as a starting point.

50
00:07:39.357 --> 00:07:50.813
Tile list
The Map Setup list is counted from the map. Layouts with different tiles each get their own list, labelled by their player counts.

51
00:07:50.813 --> 00:08:00.679
Your own counts
Turn counting off to type the counts, or mark a count as per player, which prints as “2P”.

52
00:08:00.679 --> 00:08:06.049
Notes
Add a note to any tile line.

53
00:08:06.049 --> 00:08:13.790
Heroes Placement
Say where the Heroes start. Campaigns use this for the AI Heroes too.

54
00:08:17.019 --> 00:08:29.382
AI Hero Setup
Campaigns and some cooperative Scenarios fight an AI Hero: its faction, army, AI deck, spell deck, skills and special rules. Campaigns switch this on by itself.

55
00:08:29.382 --> 00:08:31.794
Not for a Clash
Our Clash has no AI Hero, so we switch it off again.

56
00:08:33.378 --> 00:08:41.680
Victory
Write how the Scenario is won. Suggestions add a common condition as a new paragraph.

57
00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:49.693
Defeat
And how it is lost, or say there is no defeat other than the end of the last Round.

58
00:08:51.285 --> 00:09:01.598
Events by Round
Add an event and pick its Rounds: several Rounds make it repeat, listed in Round order. Then type the events or click a suggestion.

59
00:09:01.598 --> 00:09:14.233
The flood recedes
A second event in the 6th Round opens the flooded Mystical Garden, the Blocked Field we marked on the map.

60
00:09:15.833 --> 00:09:23.175
Additional Rules
Rules that change the game for this Scenario: an optional introduction and one bullet per rule.

61
00:09:23.175 --> 00:09:38.508
Own sections
Sections of your own, such as “Enemy Spawning and Movement”, follow the Additional Rules, each with a title, text and bullets.

62
00:09:39.445 --> 00:09:55.022
Markup instead of LaTeX
Every text field takes a small markup: **bold**, *italic*, :gold: for any game icon, "quotes" and -- for a dash.

63
00:09:55.022 --> 00:10:00.233
The Icon button
The Icon button opens the game icons; “Show all” lists every one. A click inserts its :name:.

64
00:10:00.233 --> 00:10:09.560
Rendered on the page
The page shows bold text, the dash, curly quotes and the gold icon, exactly as the Mission Book prints them.

65
00:10:10.487 --> 00:10:20.522
Undo and redo
Removed something by mistake? Undo it with the arrow buttons, or Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y.

66
00:10:20.522 --> 00:10:25.463
Autosave
Every change is saved in this browser as you work. Clearing the browser's data clears it too, so keep a file.

67
00:10:25.463 --> 00:10:35.998
Save file
Save file downloads the project as .scenario.json. Open it on the start screen to keep editing, on any computer.

68
00:10:35.998 --> 00:10:41.360
Back to the start
The logo takes you to the start screen. The project is listed under Continue; click it to carry on.

69
00:10:43.049 --> 00:10:51.796
Checklist
The checklist lists what is missing (Fix) and what a reviewer would ask about (Check), with a link to the step.

70
00:10:51.796 --> 00:11:08.830
Capitalisation lint
It also runs the Mission Book's capitalisation check: game terms such as Hero, Field or Round are written with a capital.

71
00:11:08.830 --> 00:11:12.582
Fixed
With “Round”, “Hero” and “Field” capitalised, the checklist is clear.

72
00:11:12.582 --> 00:11:18.729
Page preview
The Preview shows the full Mission Book page, map included.

73
00:11:18.729 --> 00:11:29.030
LaTeX
The LaTeX tab shows the .tex file the app writes, a Copy button, and the line to register it in main.tex.

74
00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:39.018
How to submit
The submit guide: where each file goes, the main.tex line, how to build, and the pull request. It is also in the ZIP.

75
00:11:39.965 --> 00:11:46.901
The ZIP bundle
One click builds the whole bundle: the .tex, each layout's map PNG and .map file, the PDF, the project file and HOW-TO-SUBMIT.md.

76
00:11:46.901 --> 00:11:52.113
PDF
The PDF lays out the Scenario like the book, to print or share.

77
00:11:52.113 --> 00:11:58.350
Single files
Or download single files: the .tex, the project file, and each layout's .map and PNG below.

78
00:11:58.799 --> 00:12:10.808
Exported files
The ZIP mirrors the Mission Book repository: the .tex goes to draft-scenarios/, the map PNGs to assets/maps/, the .map files to assets/map-files/. HOW-TO-SUBMIT.md has the rest.

79
00:12:10.808 --> 00:12:15.816
Happy scenario writing!
Write, lay out the map, check, export, and open a pull request to the Mission Book.
